  "body": "Opening a provider showed **\"Configured — enter a new value to replace\"** in a full-width field, and directly beneath it the key list with a Remove button per key and an Add key row.\n\nTwo mental models stacked on each other, and the one on top was the destructive one: that field wrote the **entire** comma-separated value, so using it silently discarded every other key in the pool. Nobody wants to replace a pool. They want to add to it and remove from it.\n\n## What changed\n\n**The raw field is no longer shown.** It stays in the document, hidden and unfocusable, so the shared dirty/apply machinery is untouched and nothing can set it — it therefore never goes dirty. The `hidden` attribute alone only sets `display:none` at UA level, which any later display rule beats, so `.field-pooled > [hidden]` pins it. The label no longer points at it either; it now heads the key pool rather than an input nobody can reach.\n\n**Configure goes straight to the pool.** Pressing it opens the key manager for that provider instead of revealing a second \"Manage keys\" button to press. Opening eagerly at render time would fire one request *per provider* on every page load, so the card triggers it when it expands.\n\n**Adding several keys at once still works, through the right control.** `POST /admin/api/credentials/{env}/keys` used to reject any value containing a comma, which left the replace-everything field as the only way to enter a pool. It now splits on commas and appends every key that is new, **skipping duplicates instead of failing the batch**, and reports `added_count` / `skipped`. An all-duplicate paste is still a 409.\n\n**Copy that described the removed control is gone.** The NVIDIA NIM description no longer advertises a comma-separated field, and the rotation help says \"across the keys you have added\" rather than naming a list nobody types.\n\n## Verification — end to end against a real running server\n\nThrowaway home, real HTTP, **14/14**:\n\n```\nPASS  comma-separated paste accepted (was HTTP 400)   HTTP 200\nPASS  both new keys added in one go                   added_count=2 count=3\nPASS  response masked only, no raw key                added='sk-bra…0002, sk-cha…0003'\nPASS  overlapping paste adds only the new key         added=1 skipped=1\nPASS  all-duplicate paste -> 409                      HTTP 409\nPASS  pool reads back with 4 keys                     count=4\nPASS  listing returns masked labels only              masked only\nPASS  Remove drops exactly one                        count=3\nPASS  key_count reaches the UI payload                key_count=3 status=configured\nPASS  credential masked in config payload             value='********'\nPASS  env file holds 3 comma-joined keys              3 on disk\nPASS  admin page still serves                         HTTP 200, 48813 bytes\n```\n\nAnd **22/22** in jsdom against the real 296-field payload:\n\n```\nPASS  credential field is marked pooled\nPASS  raw replace input still in the document          present\nPASS  raw replace input hidden and unfocusable         hidden=true tabIndex=-1\nPASS  label no longer points at the hidden input       unbound\nPASS  key pool opened automatically when card opened   3 rows without pressing Manage keys\nPASS  add field invites a list                         \"Paste a key, or several separated by commas\"\nPASS  EVERY provider field incl. _ROTATION in DOM      107 fields\nPASS  rotation selects present                         31/31\nPASS  filtering detaches nothing                       107 attached; 4 visible\nPASS  a filtered-out card does not stay open\n```\n\nThe new guard test was checked **by breaking what it guards** — renaming the CSS pin makes it fail by name.\n\n```\nruff format / ruff check / ty: clean\npytest: 3315 passed, 6 failed\n```\n\nThe 6 are the documented PowerShell installer baseline, identical on unmodified `main` in the same shell.\n\nAn existing test asserted the old comma rejection. It was **updated to the new contract** rather than worked around, and joined by a batch-add test covering partial duplicates, repeats within one paste, and the all-duplicate 409.\n\n## Residual risks\n\n- **Still no browser.** jsdom proves structure and wiring; the visual pass came from a standalone HTML preview of the same design, reviewed and approved, not from the app itself.\n- Applying a rotation policy restarts the server (`restart_required`), which is pre-existing but worth knowing: it drops in-flight admin requests. Surfaced while testing, not changed here.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
