    "message": "fix(admin): a provider credential is a pool, so stop offering to replace it\n\nOpening a provider showed \"Configured - enter a new value to replace\" in a\nfull-width field, and directly beneath it the key list with a Remove button\nper key and an Add key row. Two mental models stacked on top of each other,\nand the one on top was the destructive one: that field wrote the entire\ncomma-separated value, so using it silently discarded every other key in the\npool. Nobody wants to replace a pool. They want to add to it and remove\nfrom it.\n\nThe raw field is no longer shown. It stays in the document, hidden and\nunfocusable, so the shared dirty/apply machinery is untouched and nothing can\nset it -- it therefore never goes dirty. The `hidden` attribute alone only\nsets display:none at UA level, which any later display rule beats, so\n`.field-pooled > [hidden]` pins it. The label no longer points at it either;\nit now heads the key pool instead of an input nobody can reach.\n\nConfigure goes straight to the pool. Pressing it opens the key manager for\nthat provider rather than revealing a second \"Manage keys\" button to press.\nOpening eagerly at render time would have fired one request per provider on\nevery page load, so the card triggers it when it expands.\n\nAdding several keys at once still works, but through the right control.\nPOST /admin/api/credentials/{env}/keys used to reject any value containing a\ncomma, which left the replace-everything field as the only way to enter a\npool. It now splits on commas and appends every key that is new, skipping\nduplicates instead of failing the batch, and reports added_count and skipped.\nAn all-duplicate paste is still a 409. The add placeholder says so: \"Paste a\nkey, or several separated by commas\".\n\nCopy that described the removed control is gone: the NVIDIA NIM description\nno longer advertises a comma-separated field, and the rotation help now says\n\"across the keys you have added\" rather than naming a list nobody types.\n\nVerification, end to end against a REAL running server on a throwaway home,\n14 of 14:\n\n  comma-separated paste accepted (HTTP 400 before) -> HTTP 200, 2 keys added\n  overlapping paste added 1, skipped 1; all-duplicate paste -> 409\n  pool read back with 4 keys, Remove dropped exactly one\n  responses carried masked labels only; no raw key in any body\n  key_count reached the config payload (3, status=configured)\n  the .env the server wrote held 3 comma-joined keys\n  admin page still served 200\n\nAnd 22 of 22 in jsdom against the real 296-field payload, including the\nassertions that matter here:\n\n  credential field marked pooled; raw input present but hidden, tabIndex -1\n  label unbound from the hidden input\n  key pool opened by itself when the card opened -- 3 rows, 3 Remove buttons\n  add field invites a comma-separated list\n  all 107 provider fields incl. 31 rotation selects still in the document\n  filtering detaches nothing; a filtered-out card does not stay expanded\n\nThe new guard test was checked by breaking what it guards: renaming the CSS\npin makes it fail by name.\n\n  ruff format / ruff check / ty: clean\n  pytest: 3315 passed, 6 failed -- the documented PowerShell installer\n  baseline, identical on unmodified main in the same shell\n\nAn existing test asserted the old comma rejection; it was updated to the new\ncontract rather than worked around, and joined by a batch-add test covering\npartial duplicates, repeats within one paste, and the all-duplicate 409.\n\nNot done: still no browser. jsdom proves structure and wiring; the visual\npass came from a standalone HTML preview of the same design, not the app.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
